7. Anti-Aberration Rules
These rules are absolute.
7.1 Prohibited methods
Do not use:
* generators to produce paragraphs or exercises;
* loops that manufacture educational text;
* templated mass rewriting;
* scripts that paraphrase content;
* scripts that create exercise variants;
* scripts that populate lesson objects;
* bulk search-and-replace for substantive prose;
* programmatic translation;
* programmatic “simplification” of sentences;
* automatic generation of distractors, hints, explanations, or feedback;
* copy-paste adaptation across sections without manual rewriting;
* synthetic filler added only to meet a length or item-count target.
7.2 Permitted technical automation
Automation is allowed only for mechanical validation, such as:
* type checking;
* linting;
* unit tests;
* code execution;
* output comparison;
* schema validation;
* duplicate detection;
* link checking;
* Markdown rendering checks;
* Spanish-quality measurement;
* answer-key distribution checks;
* Playwright rendering and accessibility checks.
Validation automation must never manufacture the corrected educational content.
7.3 Required human-quality craftsmanship
Every paragraph, explanation, code comment, example, exercise, hint, feedback block, self-check question, distractor, rubric criterion, and transition must be written deliberately by hand.
Each unit must have a clear pedagogical purpose.
Prefer:
* fewer, stronger exercises;
* shorter, clearer instructions;
* realistic misconceptions;
* explicit reasoning;
* professionally edited Spanish;
* evidence-bearing scenarios;
* meaningful transfer.
Do not preserve unnecessary repetition merely to maintain the existing number of exercises.
7.4 Detection and self-correction
If an agent notices that it is:
* creating a content generator;
* writing a loop to manufacture lesson text;
* converting a report mechanically into replacements;
* repeating one exercise template across many concepts;
* producing suspiciously uniform prose;
* using a bulk mechanism to create question variants,
it must:
1. stop immediately;
2. discard that generated content;
3. return to the source and audits;
4. rewrite each affected unit manually;
5. disclose the discarded approach in its internal worklog entry.
Violation is a critical failure.

